From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 12:54:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] system/cpus: run_on_cpu under record/replay: release the
 replay mutex while the VM is stopped

Under record/replay the main loop holds the replay mutex with the BQL. A vCPU thread that has
started running takes the replay mutex before serving queued work, so synchronous work requested
from the main loop (x86 vapic_post_load on a loadvm) deadlocks. Let the vCPU have the mutex while
we wait and re-take both in lock order (BQL off, replay, BQL).

Only while the VM is stopped (!runstate_is_running(); loadvm stops it first): the virtual clock is
frozen and no vCPU runs guest code, so the vCPU thread cannot interleave timer or guest events into
the replay log while it holds the mutex. A caller with the VM running keeps the stock behaviour.
Trace event run_on_cpu_replay_release marks each hand-over.

Locking contract: stock do_run_on_cpu already drops the BQL while it waits; what changes is that
the replay mutex is released too, for main-loop run_on_cpu calls under record/replay with the VM
stopped. Test: record from a -loadvm'd snapshot (observe exp.py twoproc, -trace
run_on_cpu_replay_release) and replay twice: the release fires in the record (3x) and the
replays (6x); consoles and rrdig digests identical. From observe's rr spike (gate/rrux).
---
 system/cpus.c       | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 system/trace-events |  1 +
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/system/cpus.c b/system/cpus.c
index 97e5a5edee23b70d8bd170c2b984568f4e804672..5f5310fdd35e4e6f30ee075b261546c6575e1a0d 100644
--- a/system/cpus.c
+++ b/system/cpus.c
@@ -438,6 +438,29 @@ void qemu_init_cpu_loop(void)
 
 void run_on_cpu(CPUState *cpu, run_on_cpu_func func, run_on_cpu_data data)
 {
+    /*
+     * rrux: under record/replay the main loop holds the replay mutex while
+     * it holds the BQL.  A vCPU thread that has started running takes the
+     * replay mutex before it serves queued work (tcg-accel-ops-rr.c), so
+     * synchronous work requested from the main loop (x86 vapic_post_load on
+     * a loadvm after start, e.g. x-rr-begin-record) deadlocks.  Let the
+     * vCPU have the mutex while we wait; re-take it in lock order.
+     *
+     * Only while the VM is stopped (loadvm stops it first): the virtual
+     * clock is frozen and no vCPU can run guest code, so handing over the
+     * mutex cannot interleave timer or guest events into the replay log.
+     * A caller with the VM running keeps the stock behaviour.
+     */
+    if (replay_mode != REPLAY_MODE_NONE && !current_cpu &&
+        !runstate_is_running() && replay_mutex_locked() && bql_locked()) {
+        trace_run_on_cpu_replay_release(cpu->cpu_index);
+        replay_mutex_unlock();
+        do_run_on_cpu(cpu, func, data, &bql);
+        bql_unlock();
+        replay_mutex_lock();
+        bql_lock();
+        return;
+    }
     do_run_on_cpu(cpu, func, data, &bql);
 }
 
diff --git a/system/trace-events b/system/trace-events
index 51b4a4679a21079d25cef0f630672a1389c73525..40f385abe2be6949e140072abc3916891693de3e 100644
--- a/system/trace-events
+++ b/system/trace-events
@@ -43,6 +43,7 @@ subpage_write(void *subpage, unsigned len, uint64_t addr, uint64_t value) "subpa
 
 # cpus.c
 vm_stop_flush_all(int ret) "ret %d"
+run_on_cpu_replay_release(int cpu_index) "cpu %d"
 
 # vl.c
 vm_state_notify(int running, int reason, const char *reason_str) "running %d reason %d (%s)"
